From: LP desktop <lp@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] backend/drm: virtio-gpu cursor plane (cursor hotspots), clip FB_DAMAGE_CLIPS

Applies to Debian's wlroots 0.15.1-6, after debian/patches/series.
Built by build-wlroots.sh; see README-wlroots.md.

1. Cursor hotspots - backport of upstream e3bd3185 "drm/backend: add
   support for cursor hotspots" (wlroots 0.18, Ian Forbes).
   Linux 6.8+ hides the cursor plane of virtualized drivers (virtio-gpu,
   vmwgfx, qxl, vboxvideo) from atomic clients that have not set
   DRM_CLIENT_CAP_CURSOR_PLANE_HOTSPOT, because the host needs the
   hotspot to line its own pointer up with the guest's. Without the
   plane, output_pick_cursor_format() logs "Failed to get cursor display
   formats" and every pointer motion is a software-cursor frame.
   - drm.c: set the cap right after DRM_CLIENT_CAP_ATOMIC (before the
     planes are enumerated); atomic only - the legacy path is untouched.
     OFF unless WLR_DRM_CURSOR_HOTSPOT=1: under UTM (QEMU, x86_64
     emulation, virtio-gpu) the host-drawn cursor came out garbled and
     mirrored, and the host pointer it warped to follow the plane fed
     back into the guest's absolute pointer - the pointer moved by itself
     and clicks landed elsewhere. The software cursor is the safe default
     in a VM; real GPUs expose their cursor plane without the cap.
     The define is missing from libdrm < 2.4.118 (bookworm has 2.4.114),
     so it is provided (value 6, include/uapi/drm/drm.h).
   - properties.[ch]: HOTSPOT_X / HOTSPOT_Y in the plane property table
     (the table is bsearch()ed; it stays sorted).
   - atomic.c: when the cursor plane has both properties, set them from
     the connector's cursor hotspot. CRTC_X/Y are unchanged: the pointer
     position minus the hotspot, i.e. the image's top-left corner.

2. FB_DAMAGE_CLIPS - 0.15.1 already sends the frame's damage (upstream
   added it in 0.15), but unclipped. Backport of upstream 4339c37f
   "backend/drm: clip FB damage" (0.17) and e8e76dc2 "backend/drm: check
   whether clipped damage is empty" (0.19): the kernel rejects the whole
   atomic commit with EINVAL ("invalid damage clip") when a clip exceeds
   the FB, so the damage is intersected with the primary FB's bounds and
   no blob is sent when nothing is left. The blob is still created per
   commit (test and real) and destroyed after it.

3. The pixman renderer also offers DRM_FORMAT_MOD_LINEAR (upstream does
   from 0.16). The DRM backend gives a cursor plane of a driver without
   modifiers LINEAR only; pixman offered INVALID only, the intersection
   was empty, and even with the plane back the cursor stayed software
   ("Failed to pick cursor format"). Dumb buffers are linear anyway.

4. A DRM dumb buffer allocated for a LINEAR-only format reports LINEAR,
   not INVALID: the DRM backend checks the buffer's modifier against the
   plane's formats, and the cursor plane (LINEAR only) refused it ("cannot
   be scanned out"), falling back to software again.

5. Single-buffered output swapchain on virtual GPUs (LP-only, not
   upstream). The swapchain alternated two dumb buffers, so every commit
   carried a new FB, and virtio-gpu (Linux 6.8+, virtgpu_plane.c) ignores
   FB_DAMAGE_CLIPS when the FB changes: each frame - with the software
   cursor, each pointer motion - was a full-screen TRANSFER_TO_HOST_2D +
   RESOURCE_FLUSH (8 MB at 1080p), which under TCG stalls QEMU's main
   thread. For DRM outputs on virtio_gpu, vmwgfx, qxl and cirrus (by
   drmGetVersion name) drawn into dumb buffers (the pixman renderer's DRM
   dumb allocator; WLR_DRM_SINGLE_BUFFER=1/0 forces it) the swapchain
   hands out the last submitted buffer again (age 1) even while it is the
   plane's current FB, so the FB stays the same and only the damage is
   uploaded. These drivers scan out a host-side copy, refreshed from the
   dumb buffer on commit, so drawing into the committed buffer between
   commits does not show. It needs a kernel that honours the damage clips
   of a commit whose FB did not change: Linux 6.12 kept ignoring them
   after the first FB change (LP's kernel/patches/0001-drm-clear-
   ignore_damage_clips-on-duplicate.patch). GLES2 on virgl (GBM buffers:
   the host displays the rendered resource itself, there is no copy),
   real GPUs and bochs-drm (scanout from VRAM, the flip is free) keep
   double buffering.

6. One pixel of damage for a commit that changed nothing (LP-only,
   WLR_DRM_MIN_DAMAGE=1). Moving a hardware cursor is an atomic commit
   that also carries the primary plane with its unchanged FB and no
   damage, and a plane update without FB_DAMAGE_CLIPS is a full update to
   the kernel: virtio-gpu uploaded and flushed the whole screen for each
   pointer motion, so the cursor plane saved nothing. Such a commit now
   says (0,0)-(1,1), a pixel that is already on the screen. Only asked
   for on virtual GPUs whose FB stays the same (single buffer, 5.): a
   commit with a new FB is a full upload there regardless.

7. A cursor image drawn upside down on purpose (LP-only,
   WLR_DRM_CURSOR_FLIP_Y=1). Through virgl the host reads the cursor
   plane's buffer back with virglrenderer, which flips every image it
   reads (it takes a GL texture's bottom-up rows for granted); one that
   wayfire drew with OpenGL showed the pointer upside down at the bottom
   of its 64x64 square. The whole buffer is drawn flipped vertically, so
   the host's flip puts it back, hotspot and all. pixman's cursors
   (sway) arrive upright and are left alone.

8. The cursor plane moved by itself (LP-only, WLR_DRM_CURSOR_DIRECT=1).
   0.15 moves the cursor plane in the next frame, and asks the
   compositor for one; wayfire then renders a whole new frame into its
   other buffer, and on virtio-gpu every motion of the mouse was a flush
   of the whole screen for the host to redraw. With the variable set, a
   pointer motion is an atomic commit of the cursor plane's CRTC_X/Y
   alone (nonblocking, no event); when the plane is not up or a page flip
   is pending, the frame is asked for as before.

9. Frame events paced to the refresh rate on GPUs without vblank
   (LP-only; WLR_DRM_FRAME_PACE=1/0 forces it on/off). virtio-gpu, qxl,
   bochs, cirrus, vmwgfx, vboxvideo, hyperv_drm and simpledrm complete a
   page flip as soon as the commit is queued for the host, so the frame
   event after it came at once, and a compositor with anything moving
   drew as fast as the CPU allowed: hundreds of frames a second queued up
   for a host that shows sixty, the compositor blocked on the full queue
   and not reading input meanwhile. Under KVM with SPICE and OpenGL that
   was flicker, and a pointer, clicks and new windows that lagged behind.
   drm.c holds the next frame event until one refresh period (the mode's;
   60 Hz without one) after the previous one, as mutter's frame clock
   does. A real GPU's flip completes at vblank and never waits there.

---
diff --git a/backend/drm/atomic.c b/backend/drm/atomic.c
--- a/backend/drm/atomic.c
+++ b/backend/drm/atomic.c
@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@
 #include <stdlib.h>
+#include <string.h>
 #include <wlr/util/log.h>
 #include <xf86drm.h>
 #include <xf86drmMode.h>
@@ -103,6 +104,39 @@
 	return true;
 }
 
+static bool create_fb_damage_clips_blob(struct wlr_drm_backend *drm,
+		struct wlr_drm_fb *fb, const pixman_region32_t *damage,
+		uint32_t *blob_id) {
+	*blob_id = 0;
+	if (fb == NULL) {
+		return true;
+	}
+
+	// The kernel rejects the whole commit (EINVAL) if a clip exceeds the FB,
+	// so clip the damage (buffer-local coordinates) to the FB bounds.
+	pixman_region32_t clipped;
+	pixman_region32_init(&clipped);
+	pixman_region32_intersect_rect(&clipped, (pixman_region32_t *)damage,
+		0, 0, fb->wlr_buf->width, fb->wlr_buf->height);
+
+	int rects_len;
+	const pixman_box32_t *rects = pixman_region32_rectangles(&clipped, &rects_len);
+
+	int ret = 0;
+	if (rects_len > 0) {
+		ret = drmModeCreatePropertyBlob(drm->fd, rects,
+			sizeof(*rects) * rects_len, blob_id);
+	}
+	pixman_region32_fini(&clipped);
+	if (ret != 0) {
+		wlr_log_errno(WLR_ERROR, "Failed to create FB_DAMAGE_CLIPS property blob");
+		*blob_id = 0;
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	return true;
+}
+
 static void commit_blob(struct wlr_drm_backend *drm,
 		uint32_t *current, uint32_t next) {
 	if (*current == next) {
@@ -163,6 +197,19 @@
 	atom->failed = true;
 }
 
+static bool want_min_damage(void) {
+	static int want = -1;
+	if (want < 0) {
+		const char *env = getenv("WLR_DRM_MIN_DAMAGE");
+		want = env != NULL && strcmp(env, "1") == 0;
+	}
+	return want;
+}
+
+static bool supports_cursor_hotspots(const struct wlr_drm_plane *plane) {
+	return plane->props.hotspot_x && plane->props.hotspot_y;
+}
+
 static bool atomic_crtc_commit(struct wlr_drm_connector *conn,
 		const struct wlr_drm_connector_state *state, uint32_t flags,
 		bool test_only) {
@@ -201,14 +248,24 @@
 
 	uint32_t fb_damage_clips = 0;
 	if ((state->base->committed & WLR_OUTPUT_STATE_DAMAGE) &&
-			pixman_region32_not_empty((pixman_region32_t *)&state->base->damage) &&
 			crtc->primary->props.fb_damage_clips != 0) {
-		int rects_len;
-		const pixman_box32_t *rects = pixman_region32_rectangles(
-			(pixman_region32_t *)&state->base->damage, &rects_len);
-		if (drmModeCreatePropertyBlob(drm->fd, rects,
-				sizeof(*rects) * rects_len, &fb_damage_clips) != 0) {
+		create_fb_damage_clips_blob(drm, plane_get_next_fb(crtc->primary),
+			&state->base->damage, &fb_damage_clips);
+	}
+	// LP: a commit that changes nothing on the primary plane - the cursor
+	// plane moved, nothing else - carries no damage, and no FB_DAMAGE_CLIPS
+	// means "all of it" to the kernel: virtio-gpu then uploads and flushes
+	// the whole screen for every pointer motion. With WLR_DRM_MIN_DAMAGE=1
+	// such a commit says one pixel instead (still drawn, so resending it
+	// changes nothing on the screen).
+	if (fb_damage_clips == 0 && active && want_min_damage() &&
+			crtc->primary->props.fb_damage_clips != 0 &&
+			plane_get_next_fb(crtc->primary) != NULL) {
+		pixman_box32_t one = { .x1 = 0, .y1 = 0, .x2 = 1, .y2 = 1 };
+		if (drmModeCreatePropertyBlob(drm->fd, &one, sizeof(one),
+				&fb_damage_clips) != 0) {
 			wlr_log_errno(WLR_ERROR, "Failed to create FB_DAMAGE_CLIPS property blob");
+			fb_damage_clips = 0;
 		}
 	}
 
@@ -254,6 +311,15 @@
 			if (drm_connector_is_cursor_visible(conn)) {
 				set_plane_props(&atom, drm, crtc->cursor, crtc->id,
 					conn->cursor_x, conn->cursor_y);
+				// CRTC_X/Y stay the top-left corner of the cursor image
+				// (pointer position minus hotspot); HOTSPOT_X/Y tell a
+				// virtualized driver where the pointer is inside it.
+				if (supports_cursor_hotspots(crtc->cursor)) {
+					atomic_add(&atom, crtc->cursor->id,
+						crtc->cursor->props.hotspot_x, conn->cursor_hotspot_x);
+					atomic_add(&atom, crtc->cursor->id,
+						crtc->cursor->props.hotspot_y, conn->cursor_hotspot_y);
+				}
 			} else {
 				plane_disable(&atom, crtc->cursor);
 			}
diff --git a/backend/drm/drm.c b/backend/drm/drm.c
--- a/backend/drm/drm.c
+++ b/backend/drm/drm.c
@@ -31,6 +31,11 @@
 #include "render/wlr_renderer.h"
 #include "util/signal.h"
 
+#ifndef DRM_CLIENT_CAP_CURSOR_PLANE_HOTSPOT
+// Linux 6.8+ (include/uapi/drm/drm.h); libdrm < 2.4.118 lacks the define
+#define DRM_CLIENT_CAP_CURSOR_PLANE_HOTSPOT 6
+#endif
+
 static const uint32_t SUPPORTED_OUTPUT_STATE =
 	WLR_OUTPUT_STATE_BACKEND_OPTIONAL |
 	WLR_OUTPUT_STATE_BUFFER |
@@ -85,6 +90,18 @@
 		wlr_log(WLR_DEBUG, "Using atomic DRM interface");
 		drm->iface = &atomic_iface;
 	}
+	// Virtualized drivers (virtio-gpu, vmwgfx, qxl, vboxvideo) hide their
+	// cursor plane from atomic clients that don't promise to set the cursor
+	// hotspot (Linux 6.8+). LP asks for it only with WLR_DRM_CURSOR_HOTSPOT=1:
+	// under UTM the host drew the plane's cursor garbled, and the host
+	// pointer it moved to match fed back into the guest's, so the pointer
+	// wandered by itself and clicks missed. Without the cap the cursor is
+	// software-drawn, as before. Must happen before the planes are enumerated.
+	const char *hotspot = getenv("WLR_DRM_CURSOR_HOTSPOT");
+	if (hotspot && strcmp(hotspot, "1") == 0 && drm->iface == &atomic_iface &&
+			drmSetClientCap(drm->fd, DRM_CLIENT_CAP_CURSOR_PLANE_HOTSPOT, 1) == 0) {
+		wlr_log(WLR_INFO, "DRM_CLIENT_CAP_CURSOR_PLANE_HOTSPOT supported");
+	}
 
 	int ret = drmGetCap(drm->fd, DRM_CAP_TIMESTAMP_MONOTONIC, &cap);
 	drm->clock = (ret == 0 && cap == 1) ? CLOCK_MONOTONIC : CLOCK_REALTIME;
@@ -841,6 +858,42 @@
 	return true;
 }
 
+/*
+ * LP: WLR_DRM_CURSOR_DIRECT=1 - move the cursor plane on its own, with a
+ * commit of nothing else. Otherwise a pointer motion asks the compositor
+ * for a frame, and wayfire answers with a whole new frame in its other
+ * buffer: on virtio-gpu that is a flush of the whole screen for every
+ * motion of the mouse, which the host (virt-manager's SPICE with OpenGL)
+ * redraws - where GNOME's pointer sends nothing but the cursor's place.
+ * When the plane is not up, or a page flip is still pending (EBUSY), the
+ * frame is asked for as before, and it carries the position.
+ */
+static bool move_cursor_plane_now(struct wlr_drm_connector *conn) {
+	static int want = -1;
+	if (want < 0) {
+		const char *env = getenv("WLR_DRM_CURSOR_DIRECT");
+		want = env != NULL && strcmp(env, "1") == 0;
+	}
+	struct wlr_drm_backend *drm = conn->backend;
+	struct wlr_drm_plane *plane = conn->crtc ? conn->crtc->cursor : NULL;
+	if (!want || drm->iface != &atomic_iface || plane == NULL ||
+			plane->current_fb == NULL || plane->props.crtc_x == 0 ||
+			plane->props.crtc_y == 0 || !drm_connector_is_cursor_visible(conn)) {
+		return false;
+	}
+	drmModeAtomicReq *req = drmModeAtomicAlloc();
+	if (req == NULL) {
+		return false;
+	}
+	bool ok = drmModeAtomicAddProperty(req, plane->id, plane->props.crtc_x,
+			(uint64_t)(int64_t)conn->cursor_x) >= 0 &&
+		drmModeAtomicAddProperty(req, plane->id, plane->props.crtc_y,
+			(uint64_t)(int64_t)conn->cursor_y) >= 0 &&
+		drmModeAtomicCommit(drm->fd, req, DRM_MODE_ATOMIC_NONBLOCK, NULL) == 0;
+	drmModeAtomicFree(req);
+	return ok;
+}
+
 static bool drm_connector_move_cursor(struct wlr_output *output,
 		int x, int y) {
 	struct wlr_drm_connector *conn = get_drm_connector_from_output(output);
@@ -867,6 +920,9 @@
 	conn->cursor_x = box.x;
 	conn->cursor_y = box.y;
 
+	if (move_cursor_plane_now(conn)) {
+		return true;
+	}
 	wlr_output_update_needs_frame(output);
 	return true;
 }
@@ -1449,6 +1505,92 @@
 	return 1000000000000LL / mhz;
 }
 
+/*
+ * LP: frame events at most once a refresh period on GPUs without vblank.
+ *
+ * virtio-gpu (and the other virtual GPUs below) have no vblank: the
+ * kernel sends the page-flip event as soon as the commit has been queued
+ * for the host, not when the host has shown it. The frame event that
+ * follows it then came at once, and a compositor with anything moving -
+ * an animation, a window being dragged, the pointer drawn in software -
+ * drew as fast as the CPU could: hundreds of frames a second that the
+ * host could not show, queued behind each other in the virtqueue, the
+ * compositor blocked on a full queue and not reading input meanwhile.
+ * Under KVM with SPICE and OpenGL that was the screen flickering, and the
+ * pointer, clicks and new windows all lagging behind. mutter paces its
+ * frames with its own clock at the mode's refresh rate; this does the
+ * same here: the next frame event is held until one refresh period after
+ * the previous one. A real GPU's flip completes at vblank, so it never
+ * waits here. WLR_DRM_FRAME_PACE=1/0 forces it on/off.
+ */
+static bool want_frame_pacing(struct wlr_drm_backend *drm) {
+	if (drm->frame_pace == 0) {
+		drm->frame_pace = 1;
+		const char *env = getenv("WLR_DRM_FRAME_PACE");
+		if (env != NULL && env[0] != '\0') {
+			drm->frame_pace = strcmp(env, "1") == 0 ? 2 : 1;
+		} else {
+			static const char *const virtual_gpus[] = {
+				"virtio_gpu", "qxl", "bochs-drm", "bochs", "cirrus",
+				"cirrus-qemu", "vmwgfx", "vboxvideo", "hyperv_drm",
+				"simpledrm", NULL,
+			};
+			drmVersion *version = drmGetVersion(drm->fd);
+			for (size_t i = 0; version != NULL && virtual_gpus[i] != NULL; i++) {
+				if (strcmp(version->name, virtual_gpus[i]) == 0) {
+					drm->frame_pace = 2;
+				}
+			}
+			drmFreeVersion(version);
+		}
+		if (drm->frame_pace == 2) {
+			wlr_log(WLR_INFO, "Frame events paced to the refresh rate");
+		}
+	}
+	return drm->frame_pace == 2;
+}
+
+static int handle_frame_pace_timer(void *data) {
+	struct wlr_drm_connector *conn = data;
+	clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &conn->last_frame);
+	if (conn->backend->session->active &&
+			conn->status == WLR_DRM_CONN_CONNECTED && conn->crtc != NULL) {
+		wlr_output_send_frame(&conn->output);
+	}
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static void send_frame(struct wlr_drm_connector *conn) {
+	struct wlr_drm_backend *drm = conn->backend;
+	if (!want_frame_pacing(drm)) {
+		wlr_output_send_frame(&conn->output);
+		return;
+	}
+	int refresh = conn->output.refresh > 0 ? conn->output.refresh : 60000;
+	int64_t period = 1000000000000LL / refresh;
+	struct timespec now;
+	clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &now);
+	int64_t since = (int64_t)(now.tv_sec - conn->last_frame.tv_sec) * 1000000000LL +
+		(now.tv_nsec - conn->last_frame.tv_nsec);
+	if (since >= period) {
+		conn->last_frame = now;
+		wlr_output_send_frame(&conn->output);
+		return;
+	}
+	if (conn->frame_pace_timer == NULL) {
+		conn->frame_pace_timer = wl_event_loop_add_timer(
+			wl_display_get_event_loop(drm->display),
+			handle_frame_pace_timer, conn);
+		if (conn->frame_pace_timer == NULL) {
+			conn->last_frame = now;
+			wlr_output_send_frame(&conn->output);
+			return;
+		}
+	}
+	int ms = (int)((period - since + 999999) / 1000000);
+	wl_event_source_timer_update(conn->frame_pace_timer, ms > 0 ? ms : 1);
+}
+
 static void handle_page_flip(int fd, unsigned seq,
 		unsigned tv_sec, unsigned tv_usec, unsigned crtc_id, void *data) {
 	struct wlr_drm_backend *drm = data;
@@ -1511,7 +1653,7 @@
 	wlr_output_send_present(&conn->output, &present_event);
 
 	if (drm->session->active) {
-		wlr_output_send_frame(&conn->output);
+		send_frame(conn);
 	}
 }
 
@@ -1544,6 +1686,9 @@
 
 void destroy_drm_connector(struct wlr_drm_connector *conn) {
 	disconnect_drm_connector(conn);
+	if (conn->frame_pace_timer != NULL) {
+		wl_event_source_remove(conn->frame_pace_timer);
+	}
 
 	wl_list_remove(&conn->link);
 	free(conn);
